# Text-based 3-month category trend

## Context

Categories currently show a single month's target-vs-actual (Categories tab) or a month-over-month bar chart (Month Summary tab), but there's no way to see whether a category has been climbing or falling over several months in a row. The user wants a lightweight, text-only trend display per category, covering the 3 months ending at whatever month is currently selected, with a way to pick just a few categories at a time rather than dumping all of them into one view.

Confirmed decisions:
1. **Format**: three months' amounts joined by arrows, with a plain `^`/`v` glyph after each month showing whether it moved up or down from the prior month — e.g. `Groceries:  Jun $310.00  →  Jul $340.00 ^  →  Aug $290.00 v`. No sparkline bars, no extra statistics.
2. **Placement**: a new section on the existing Categories tab (`CategorySummaryView`), not a new tab and not the Month Summary tab.
3. **Month window**: the selected month and the two before it — shifts when the user navigates months via the existing nav bar, consistent with how the YTD section already behaves.

## Implementation

### New `CategoryTrendService.java`
Consumes `BudgetService` (calling its existing public `categoryTotals(YearMonth)` three times — once per month in the window), reusing the same income-only exclusion every other spending view already applies.

```java
public class CategoryTrendService {
    public CategoryTrendService(BudgetService budgetService) { ... }

    public List<CategoryTrend> trendsThroughMonth(YearMonth endingMonth) {
        // firstMonth = endingMonth.minusMonths(2), secondMonth = endingMonth.minusMonths(1)
        // look up each category's actual in firstMonth/secondMonth by name (defaulting to
        // ZERO if a category has no transactions that month — categories aren't month-scoped,
        // so the same set of categories always exists across all three months)
        // skip income-only categories (same filter BudgetService callers already use)
    }

    public record MonthAmount(YearMonth month, BigDecimal amount) {}

    public static class CategoryTrend {
        // category, List<MonthAmount> monthAmounts (exactly 3, oldest to newest)
    }
}
```

### `CategorySummaryView.java`
- New constructor param `CategoryTrendService categoryTrendService`.
- New section: a multi-select `ListView<Category>` (`SelectionMode.MULTIPLE`; relies on `Category.toString()` already returning just the name, same as existing combo boxes) populated with the categories that have trend data (i.e. the same non-income-only set `CategoryTrendService` returns), next to a `VBox` of `Label` lines — one per *selected* category — rendering the arrow-formatted trend string.
- `refresh()` re-fetches `categoryTrendService.trendsThroughMonth(selectedMonth.get())`, repopulates the `ListView`'s items, and re-renders the label lines for whatever's currently selected. A selection-change listener on the `ListView`'s `getSelectedItems()` re-renders the labels immediately when the user (de)selects a category — no extra button needed.
- New private formatting helper builds the `"{Category}:  {Mon} $amt  →  {Mon} $amt {^|v}  →  {Mon} $amt {^|v}"` string per category, comparing each month's amount to the previous one with `BigDecimal.compareTo` (`>` → `^`, `<` → `v`, equal → `=`), using `Month.getDisplayName(TextStyle.SHORT, Locale.ROOT)` for the 3-letter month abbreviation.

### `MainApp.java`
Construct `CategoryTrendService categoryTrendService = new CategoryTrendService(budgetService);` alongside `PriorityAlignmentService`, and pass it into `CategorySummaryView`'s constructor.

### Tests
- **New `CategoryTrendServiceTest.java`**: a category with transactions in all 3 months computes the right 3 amounts in the right order; a category with no transactions in an earlier month defaults that month to zero (not skipped/omitted); an income-only category is excluded entirely; a window crossing a year boundary (e.g. ending month January) still resolves the two prior months correctly (November/December of the previous year).
- No UI test file — this codebase has no tests for any JavaFX view class (`MonthSummaryView`, `TransactionEntryView`, etc. are all untested), so `CategorySummaryView`'s new section follows that existing convention.

## Critical files
- `src/main/java/com/example/finance/CategoryTrendService.java` (new)
- `src/main/java/com/example/finance/CategorySummaryView.java`
- `src/main/java/com/example/finance/MainApp.java`

## Verification
- `mvn -q test` — new `CategoryTrendServiceTest` passes alongside everything else.
- `mvn javafx:run`: on the Categories tab, select 2-3 categories from the new list and confirm each renders a 3-month arrow-formatted line; log a transaction that changes one month's spend and confirm the arrows update after navigating back to that month and refreshing; navigate months via the nav bar and confirm the trend window shifts accordingly.

